# Typed letters that stand for one German letter (fuer -> für, Strasse -> Straße).
ae ä
oe ö
ue ü
ss ß

# find-accent-misspellings.py: only ß/ss pairs are misspellings. Plain and
# umlaut forms are often both real words (mochte/möchte, bluten/Blüten).
only-marks ß

# German nouns are capitalised: find-supplement.py keeps capitalised words;
# the engine expects a capital after a word less to be a name (search.cpp:
# capitalCost).
capitalised-nouns

# find-respellings.py: the 1996 spelling reform. The list is current; older
# texts in the corpora write ß after a short vowel where it ends a word or
# comes before a consonant (daß, muß, Fluß, wußte: dass, muss, Fluss,
# wusste). Such a word is an old spelling when the list has the ss form
# and the corpus prefers it (Fuß, Spaß, groß stay: ß after a long vowel).
respell ß(?![aeiouäöüy]) ss

# Compounds (plan 6g): closed compounds of dictionary words are likely words
# (tweedehandsauto, kaputtgemacht) rather than two words with a space missing,
# unless the two words are likelier (heefteen: heeft een): `unknown` makes them
# a missing word made of known parts (compoundBonus), not words by themselves.
# Parts of 4+ letters, up to three, joined directly or by a linking letter.
compound 4 3 link=s,es,n,en,er,e unknown
